I'm also used to coding in shops with "dozens to hundreds" of 
programmers. If you don't actually adopt the idioms of the broader user 
community for the languages you choose it's much harder to hire people 
and bring them up-to-speed freeing your experienced people up for more 
tasks. Additionally, by not adopting those idioms, you can miss certain 
efficiencies precisely because they aren't reasonably achievable in all 
the languages you use.


> * you do _not_ hide information from the next
>   maintainer by deliberate terseness where a more
>   spelled out, more universally comprehensible way
>   of writing the same fragment of code is available.

Agreed. For code to be reliably maintainable, it has to be readable. But 
you also have to be able to make assumptions about the reading level of 
the people _doing_ the maintenance, and one of those assumptions should 
be that they're familiar with the idioms. Something like while(*s++ = 
*t++); may very well scare the hell out of someone who picked up "The C 
Programming Language" last week, but it's eminently readable to anyone 
experienced enough to be trusted maintaining a production project. In 
fact, it would be more readable than the verbose technique for some 
people because it's familiar.


> For a single example, your
> 
>   while(curr)
> 
> is hiding information about what the code does and
> what the compiler is expected to do that might be
> astonishingly useful to the junior maintainer taking
> your place:
> 
>   while ( ( fooListWalker != ( (FooStructLinkType) NULL ) ) == true )
> 
> (not worrying about using vertical whitespace for
> the moment) changing long puzzlement to instant
> comprehension.

You seriously believe that the second choice (aside from the variable 
name) is more readable? Not I. I look at the first and say: "Here's a 
loop that's going to terminate as soon as curr is zero. I wish they had 
named it something more expressive than curr." I look at the second and 
say: "Uh. Hold on a sec. Uh. Okay, we're iterating over something that's 
probably a linked list. Was this written by that intern that was here 
last summer?"

You could argue that I learn more about the loop from the second version 
than I do from the first, but it's nothing that I wouldn't have learned 
from the loop itself or the tactical comment that should have preceded 
the 'while' and told me what was about to happen.


